# G004 — Binary to Decimal and Back

*v2. See `binary.md` for prose: base conversion as parameterised
resolver native, choreography emerges from format negotiation between
agents with different preferred representations.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Sensor-Display Format Negotiation

[sensor-display
    IN ORDER TO
        bridge a sensor producing integers with a display expecting hex
        through a single resolver-native conversion at the boundary

    concurrent [
        @sensor{read-value}              -> @raw
    |
        @display{format}                  -> @fmt
    ]
    join

    @formatted <- [base
                    {value: @raw,
                     radix: @fmt is "hex" ? 16 : 10}]
    @display{show: @formatted}
]

---

## Verb Surface

[base
    value           {value, radix}            ;; parameterised native
]

[binary
    value           {value}                   ;; -> [bits]
]

[decimal
    value           {bits}                    ;; -> Int
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: parameterised native `where` radix picks the family

[base {value: @n, radix: @r}]
    IN ORDER TO
        name one capability that covers every base-conversion variant
        so the resolver's standard library has one entry, not one per base
]

`@binary`, `@decimal`, `@hex` could each be separate natives; `@base`
is the parameterised form that subsumes them. Same move the resolver
makes everywhere — one generic dispatch with parameters rather than
many specific verbs.

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [base {value: 255, radix: 2}]         ;; -> "11111111"
    -> [base {value: 255, radix: 16}]        ;; -> "ff"
    -> [binary {value: 42}]                   ;; -> [1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0]
    -> [decimal {bits: [1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0]}]   ;; -> 42
]
